And frankly, it get to like -20 once every year here, so I don't know what everyone's complaining about, but:

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:12.080
"Frigid polar vortex."

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:12.980
"Polar vortex."

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:14.160
"Polar vortex."

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:15.200
"Polar vortex."

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:16.100
"Polar vortex."

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:17.340
"The polar vortex."

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:21.600
"Polar vortex, you are not my buddy. I would give you the finger, but it's hiding in my Snuggie."

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:22.460
Polar vortex!

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:23.400
Polar vortex!

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:24.500
Polar vortex!

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:28.700
I've never heard of this thing! Where did it come from? Why do we have a polar vortex all the sudden?

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:34.580
Okay, well, polar vortices are cyclones of air that exist around the poles, both poles, and they're caused by

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:41.120
the temperature being different at the equator than it is at the pole, which happens, and has been happening,

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:46.440
since the world existed. All of the planets that have atmospheres have polar vortices.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:50.900
Usually, we don't get the same, little offshoot that we got that dipped down into America and sat there

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:57.220
for a little while. It was colder in Georgia than it was in Alaska. It's weird, I agree. But the weird events

00:00:57.220 --> 00:01:03.200
always make us talk about global warming, when really, this is the exact opposite of the way to think about

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:08.580
global warming. Saying that it's cold here, that means global warming doesn't exist is like saying

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:13.700
I have a calculator, which is proof that Jean Claude Van Damme eats turnips.

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:18.540
I've been thinking about this a lot, because it's super annoying and I think the problem is that global warming

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:24.600
is a terrible name for the thing that global warming is. It makes it sound like things are going to get warmer,

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:30.200
and yes, we are talking about an overall average increase in global temperatures, but the amount of

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:36.840
temperature increase that would be truly disastrous on a global scale is like, not even noticeable

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:43.640
on a local scale. If the planet heated up 4 degrees Celsius, that would be a disaster. But if your town heated

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:48.580
up 4 degrees Celsius, no one would even talk about it. The scientific community is more interested in calling

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:51.880
it global climate change because that points out that it's not just about warming.

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:55.060
All sorts of things are going to happen. It's going to get warmer in someplace and colder in other places.

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:58.200
It's not going to rain in places, like, it's going to affect the entire climate. But I think that doesn't really get

00:01:58.200 --> 00:01:59.660
at the real problem here.

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:04.020
The real problem isn't with the climate change or the warming. It's with the global.

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:09.600
We are terrible at thinking globally. it's just really- It's impossible to do. Okay, I've got an idea.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:14.100
Everybody get a pen and a piece of paper. I'm going to write down, as a fairly informed person,

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:20.220
a member of the people who think about things a lot, what percent of the world's are I think the US takes up.

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:25.000
Now I'm going to look it up. Okay, I said 5%, which I thought was like, a good underestimation of the size

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:32.200
that I think the US is. It is in fact 1.9%! That's the whole United States, including Alaska, which is really big.

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:35.760
I mean, we know that we're not the center of the universe, but do we really know that?

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:41.020
It doesn't seem like it. So here's how I think about it. I don't think about the weather when I think about

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:44.140
global warming. I don't think about the climate when I think about global warming.

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:50.120
Global warming is just an overall increase in the amount of energy trapped in the atmosphere,

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:56.460
and that energy can be trapped as evaporated water or wind or heat or hurricanes or polar vortices.

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:02.940
It's just energy and the amount of energy is increasing. No one can argue with that.

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:09.040
And as the amount of energy increases, the globe warms on average. And the climate changes.

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:14.200
Talking about it as energy separates it from that thing in my head and allows me to think about it the way that it

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:21.000
actually is, rather than applying to my everyday experiences, which have nothing to do with anything

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:27.180
global. Not that average global atmospheric energy increase is a super catchy name or anything,

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:32.040
but that;s how I think about it, I feel like people would be more accepting of the idea if we talked about it

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:37.260
as an energy increase. I could be wrong and I'm not going to change everything with a video blog.

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:39.700
But that's how I think about it, and I thought I would share.

